CAT = "maceOrders"; SEG = "ORD"
Q = [
('A medication order lists the drug name, dose, route and time but has no prescriber signature. What should the aide do?',
 'Question it before giving', ['Give it, since the rest is there', 'Give it and note no signature', 'Give half the dose for now'],
 'The model curriculum says a complete order is signed and legible and names the drug, dose, route, time and frequency. An incomplete order is questioned through the nurse.'),
('Which element is missing from this order: "Metoprolol 25 mg by mouth"?',
 'Frequency', ['The drug name', 'The dose', 'The route'],
 'A complete order names the drug, dose, route, time and frequency. This one gives no frequency, so the nurse clarifies it with the prescriber.'),
('A physician calls and asks the medication aide to start an antibiotic tonight. Under the model curriculum, what should the aide do?',
 'Get the nurse to take the call', ['Write it on the MAR now', 'Read it back and tell the nurse', 'Give a first dose now'],
 'The model curriculum says the MA-C should not take verbal or telephone orders. A licensed nurse takes and records the order.'),
('A handwritten order is hard to read and could be 10 mg or 40 mg. What should happen?',
 'The nurse clarifies it', ['The aide picks the lower dose', 'The aide guesses', 'The nurse reads it after the dose'],
 'A complete order must be legible. An order that can be read two ways is clarified before any dose, since guessing is a common cause of errors.'),
("Where are a resident's scheduled medications recorded for each dose given?",
 'The MAR', ['The controlled substance log', "The resident's care plan", 'The pharmacy delivery sheet'],
 "Orders are transcribed onto the facility's medication document, the medication administration record (MAR), where each dose given is charted."),
('Under the model curriculum, what does the medication aide record on the MAR after giving a dose?',
 'Initials and the time', ['Only a check mark', 'Initials only', 'The time only'],
 'The model curriculum lists identifying initials and the time on the MAR as the documentation for each dose given.'),
('A resident refuses her morning tablet. Under the model curriculum, how is this charted on the MAR?',
 'Circle it and note why', ['Leave the box blank', 'Initial it as given', 'Cross out the whole order'],
 'The model curriculum says the aide circles the dose and documents the reason a client did not take a medication, and the nurse is told as policy directs.'),
('When should a medication aide chart that a dose was given?',
 'Right after giving it', ['Before preparing it', 'At the end of the shift', 'When the next dose is due'],
 'Charting before giving can record a dose that never reached the resident, and waiting until the end of the shift invites missed or double doses.'),
('Which record tracks each dose of a controlled substance taken from stock?',
 'A controlled substance log', ['The MAR alone', "The resident's care plan", 'The pharmacy delivery slip'],
 'The model curriculum names a controlled substance medication log as part of the documentation system, along with the MAR, so each dose is accounted for.'),
("A medication aide counts a resident's controlled pain tablets and the number does not match the log. What should happen?",
 'Report it before going on', ['Fix the log to match', 'Report it at the next count', 'Add tablets from stock'],
 'A count that does not match may mean an error, a missed entry or diversion. It is reported at once as facility policy directs; the log is never changed to fit.'),
("How are medications on a medication cart kept when the aide walks away to a resident's room?",
 'Locked', ['Closed', 'Parked in view', 'Pushed to the wall'],
 'The model curriculum covers storage in the medication room, cart and tray. An unattended cart is locked so no one else can reach the medications.'),
('A medication label says to store the product in the refrigerator. Where should it go?',
 'The medication refrigerator', ['The staff food refrigerator', 'The locked cart drawer', 'The med room shelf'],
 'Medications that need cold storage go in the refrigerator set aside for medications, kept apart from food and drink.'),
("A resident's eye drops expired last week. What should the aide do with them?",
 'Remove them for disposal', ['Use them until the refill arrives', 'Use them if they look clear', 'Use them up'],
 'The model curriculum covers disposal of outdated, contaminated or unused medication. Expired products are removed and disposed of as facility policy directs.'),
('A tablet drops on the floor while the aide is preparing it. What should happen to that tablet?',
 'Discard it per policy', ['Wipe it and give it', 'Return it to the bottle', 'Save it in the cart for later'],
 "A dropped tablet is contaminated. It is discarded by the facility's disposal procedure and a new tablet is prepared."),
("A resident's medication was stopped by the prescriber, and some tablets are left. What should become of them?",
 'Disposal by facility policy', ['Keep them for a new resident', 'Give them until they run out', 'Keep them as stock'],
 'Unused medication is disposed of as policy directs. Giving a stopped medication or passing it to another person is an error.'),
("A family member asks how to get rid of a resident's leftover tablets at home. Which option does the FDA prefer?",
 'A drug take-back site', ['The kitchen sink drain', 'The trash bin', 'Flushing down the toilet'],
 'The FDA lists take-back locations and mail-back envelopes as the preferred option. Flushing is only for drugs on the FDA flush list.'),
('When no take-back option exists and the drug is not on the FDA flush list, how does the FDA say to put it in household trash?',
 'Mix with litter, seal in a bag', ['Crush it into a powder first', 'Drop it loose in the bin', 'Dissolve it in hot tap water'],
 'The FDA says to mix the medicine, uncrushed, with something unappealing such as dirt, cat litter or used coffee grounds, seal it in a plastic bag and throw it away.'),
('Before an empty prescription bottle goes in the trash at home, what does the FDA advise?',
 'Scratch out personal details', ['Rinse it with bleach', 'Remove the cap', 'Tape the lid shut and recycle it'],
 'The FDA advises scratching out personal information on the prescription label before discarding the empty container.'),
("A medication aide notices that a resident's new order is for the same drug she already takes, under a different name. What should the aide do?",
 'Hold it and tell the nurse', ['Give both, as ordered', 'Give both, then tell the nurse', 'Stop the older one'],
 'A brand and a generic name can be the same drug, so two orders may double the dose. The aide does not choose between orders; the nurse resolves it.'),
("Which practice keeps one resident's medications from being given to another?",
 'Separate labeled supplies', ['Pouring all doses at once', 'Sorting cups by room', 'Pouring ahead for the hall'],
 "Each resident's medications are stored and labeled for that resident. Mixing supplies makes it easy to give the wrong drug or the wrong person's dose."),
('Under the model curriculum, which action is part of questioning an incomplete medication order?',
 'Bring it to the nurse', ['Guess the missing part', 'Skip the dose quietly', 'Give it, then tell the nurse'],
 'The model curriculum asks the aide to identify an incomplete order and question it. The nurse contacts the prescriber to complete it.'),
('A label on a medication cup reads only "8 a.m. pills" with no resident name. What is the safest action?',
 'Do not give them', ['Give them to the 8 a.m. resident', 'Guess the owner', 'Give them if the count is right'],
 'Unlabeled medication cannot be identified or matched to an order. The aide prepares the doses again from labeled supplies and reports the cup as policy directs.'),
('The MAR shows a dose due at 9 a.m., but the order on the chart says 9 p.m. What should the aide do?',
 'Report it to the nurse', ['Give it at 9 a.m.', 'Tell the nurse at shift change', 'Change the MAR to 9 p.m.'],
 'A transcription error between the order and the MAR is a common source of errors. The nurse checks the order and corrects the MAR; the aide does not change it.'),
("A resident's medication is kept in its original pharmacy container. Why does this matter?",
 'The label names the drug', ['It lets the aide pour ahead', 'It allows sharing between residents', 'It replaces the MAR check'],
 'The pharmacy label names the resident, drug, strength and directions, which the aide checks against the MAR at each of the three safety checks.'),
('Which item belongs in the locked medication storage area?',
 'Oral tablets', ['Urine specimens', 'Disinfectant wipes', "Residents' snacks"],
 'The storage area holds medications only. Food, chemicals and personal items are kept elsewhere so medications stay secure and uncontaminated.'),
('A medication aide finds an unlabeled bottle of tablets in the medication room. What should happen to it?',
 'Set it aside for disposal', ['Identify it by its imprint', 'Return it to the cart stock', "Put it in the likely resident's bin"],
 "A medication that cannot be identified cannot be given. It is removed and handled by the facility's disposal procedure."),
('A resident goes out for the day and a dose will be due while she is away. Who arranges how that dose is handled?',
 'The nurse, per policy', ['The aide, alone', 'The family, as they wish', 'The pharmacist, by phone'],
 'Leave-of-absence medications follow facility policy under nursing direction. The aide does not send doses out on their own judgment.'),
('A blister card shows a dose was popped out for today, but the MAR has no initials for it. What does this most likely mean?',
 'A charting gap to report', ['The dose was refused', 'The drug was stopped', 'The card was mislabeled at pharmacy'],
 'Without initials, no one can tell if the dose reached the resident. The aide reports it so the nurse can find out before another dose is given.'),
]
